    "result_text": "A claim's period now comes from its due-by date instead of its claim date. All 4 Playwright tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\nNo database change was needed, because each claim already stores the due-by it was given when created.\n\n**Server**\n- **Adding a claim** (`ClaimService.add`): the due-by is worked out first (date plus terms). The add is refused if that due-by falls in a closed period, and the claim is a late entry if it falls in a soft-closed one. Quick-add and paste go through this same code.\n- **Changing a claim**: editing, opening, settling, voiding, approving, rejecting, deferring, splitting, moving to another person and re-settling are now refused when the claim's due-by falls in a closed period. Before, this was checked against the claim's date.\n- **Numbering**: per-period numbers (`P2-1`, `X-1`) follow the due-by.\n- **Closing a period** (`PeriodService.closeNow`): the fixed totals count the claims whose due-by falls in the period.\n\n**Front end**\n- The period shown on a claim and the per-person period figures follow the due-by, so the period totals and the summary do too.\n- The client-side \"closed period\" checks and their error messages now refer to the due-by.\n\n**Decisions for you to check**\n- **Editing and re-dating no longer check the new date.** Since the due-by never changes after creation, changing a claim's date can't move it into or out of a period. So these only check the existing due-by. It also means re-dating no longer gives the claim a new number.\n- **A split-off claim gets its own due-by check.** It gets a due-by from the terms in force now, so the split is refused if that due-by is in a closed period. It is marked a late entry if the due-by is in a soft-closed one; the test expected this mark. No separate `LATE_ENTRY` audit line is written for it, which matches the audit lines the test expects.",
